Helios DXVK fork: keep the NVX extensions on Windows behind HELIOS_DXVK_NVX

Conduit guest/nvk-rm (NVK DLSS, patches-windows/0066-0067). Applied after
0022 by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

DxvkDeviceInfo drops VK_NVX_binary_import and VK_NVX_image_view_handle
unless DXVK runs under Wine (env::isWineVulkan()). Our D3D11 UMD runs DXVK
on Windows itself, so every device lost them, and with them the CUDA interop
NGX (DLSS) uses through ID3D11VkExtDevice1/ID3D11VkExtContext1.

HELIOS_DXVK_NVX=1 (process environment) keeps them in 64-bit processes; the
Helios UMD bridge sets it for NVK processes when the `Dlss` registry knob is
on. 32-bit processes and safe mode still drop them. Default (unset): no
change.

diff --git a/src/dxvk/dxvk_device_info.cpp b/src/dxvk/dxvk_device_info.cpp
index b2c70f0b..43cb55e7 100644
--- a/src/dxvk/dxvk_device_info.cpp
+++ b/src/dxvk/dxvk_device_info.cpp
@@ -574,7 +574,13 @@ namespace dxvk {
     if (!instance.options().enableUnifiedImageLayout)
       m_featuresSupported.khrUnifiedImageLayouts.unifiedImageLayouts = VK_FALSE;
 
-    if (env::is32BitHostPlatform() || !env::isWineVulkan() || safeMode) {
+    // Helios: on Windows DXVK runs without Wine, so isWineVulkan() is always
+    // false and would hide the NVX extensions from every D3D11 device.
+    // HELIOS_DXVK_NVX=1 (set by the Helios UMD when the `Dlss` knob is on)
+    // keeps them for 64-bit processes, for NGX's CUDA interop (DLSS).
+    const bool heliosNvx = env::getEnvVar("HELIOS_DXVK_NVX") == "1";
+
+    if (env::is32BitHostPlatform() || (!env::isWineVulkan() && !heliosNvx) || safeMode) {
       // CUDA interop is unnecessary on 32-bit, no games use it. These extensions
       // can also cause device creation errors for unknown reasons.
       m_featuresSupported.nvxBinaryImport = VK_FALSE;
